Database Architecture Document
PostgreSQL Database Architecture
Prepared for: Nicolas Assignment
Based on the internship sprint planning document.

1. Introduction
This document describes the proposed PostgreSQL database architecture for a multi-tenant SaaS platform. It explains the database structure, relationships, security, and Prisma ORM integration.
2. PostgreSQL
PostgreSQL is an open-source relational database that provides ACID compliance, strong security, indexing, transactions, JSON support and scalability.
3. Prisma ORM
Prisma ORM maps application models to PostgreSQL tables, simplifies CRUD operations, migrations and type-safe queries.
4. Multi-tenancy
Each school (tenant) has isolated data linked through tenant_id. Users belong to a tenant while the platform owner can manage all tenants.
5. Core Tables
users(id,email,password_hash,role_id,tenant_id,created_at)
roles(id,name)
permissions(id,name)
role_permissions(role_id,permission_id)
refresh_tokens(id,user_id,token,expires_at)
tenants(id,name,status)
sessions(id,user_id,login_time)
password_resets(id,user_id,token,expires_at)
6. Relationships
roles 1:M users; users 1:M refresh_tokens; users 1:M sessions; roles M:M permissions via role_permissions; tenants 1:M users.
7. Security
Passwords hashed with bcrypt/Argon2, JWT authentication, HTTPS, least privilege, role-based access control, audit logging, encrypted backups.
8. ERD (Text)
tenants -> users -> sessions
          -> refresh_tokens
roles -> users
roles -> role_permissions <- permissions
9. Conclusion
The architecture supports authentication, authorization, scalability and future modules using PostgreSQL and Prisma ORM.
